id: ops-cout
title: A case opener sharing its first arm cleared the outer scope and refused a genuinely attested publish
description: "The sibling-arm reset that closed the case-arm bypass ran before the block scope was pushed. A case opener that shares a segment with its first arm both opens the block and starts an arm, so the reset cleared the parent scope instead of a fresh arm scope. A flag bound before the block became unresolvable and the auditor refused a publish that carries provenance on every path, which would block a release check."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 1
tags[3]: attestation,false-positive,regression
created_at: "2026-09-01T08:14:18.798Z"
updated_at: "2026-09-01T08:14:20.008Z"
closed_at: "2026-09-01T08:14:19.996Z"
completed_at: "2026-09-01T08:14:19.996Z"
author: claude
acceptance_criteria: A binding made before a case block survives an opener that shares its first arm; A binding made inside a case arm still cannot attest a publish after esac; Both are verified red on revert in the correct direction
risk: medium
comments[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-09-01T08:14:19.422Z",claude,"Reproduced and fixed. Before: 'FLAG=--provenance; case $X in a) : ;; esac; npm publish $FLAG' was refused although FLAG is --provenance on every path. The multi-line spelling of the same shape was accepted, which localised the defect to the opener sharing a segment with its first arm.\n\nFix: the arm reset now runs only when the segment does not itself open a scope. When it does open one, the scope pushed immediately afterwards is already empty, which is the same branch identity by construction, so clearing beforehand could only reach the outer scope.\n\nRed-on-revert, in both directions. Removing the change <= 0 guard turns 'a binding made before a case block survives an opener that shares its first arm' red while 'a binding made inside a case arm still cannot attest a publish after esac' stays green. That pairing is the point: the second test is what proves the fix did not reopen the bypass the reset exists to close, and a fix that only made the first test green would be a regression.\n\nFull sixteen-case matrix after the change: eight fail-opens still refused, eight legitimate constructions still accepted, zero mismatches. Fleet scan unchanged at 21 OK with pm-rust recognition none."
close_reason: "Arm reset deferred so a case opener sharing its first arm no longer clears the outer scope; the arm-local bypass stays closed, verified red on revert in both directions."
body: ""
